.TH CTBREC-DISK-MODE 1 "2026-11-13" "ctbrec-disk-mode" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ctbrec-disk-mode \- strict low-disk allowlist mode for ctbrec
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ctbrec-disk-mode
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ctbrec-disk-mode
runs inside the ctbrec LXC and talks to the ctbrec REST API on
.IR https://127.0.0.1:8443 .
While free space on the recordings filesystem is above the enter threshold all
models record normally. At or below the enter threshold every model that is not
on the allowlist stops recording: models that are recording at that moment are
suspended through the ctbrec API, which stops their download and keeps them
paused. Allowlisted models keep recording; an allowlisted model that was
already paused is left paused.
.P
Only models that are actually recording are suspended. An idle model uses no
disk, and every
.B suspend
call makes ctbrec take its recorder lock, so suspending hundreds of idle models
in one burst is pure risk: a single wedged stop deadlocks the recorder and every
further call piles up behind it.
.P
Suspend and resume calls are issued one at a time, in batches of at most
.B max_suspends_per_run
or
.BR max_resumes_per_run ,
with a delay between calls. The first call that fails or times out aborts the
run and starts a
.B failure_cooldown_s
backoff, so a wedged recorder is contacted once per cooldown instead of once per
minute.
.P
When free space reaches the exit threshold, only the models that this tool
suspended are resumed, in the same paced batches. The exit is latched: once
draining has started it continues on later runs even if free space falls back
between the thresholds, otherwise resuming a large set would flap around the
exit threshold. Models the user had already paused before the mode was entered
are never touched. While the mode is active, every run re\-suspends any
non\-allowlisted model that has started recording, for example a new model or
one resumed by hand in the WebUI, and re\-admits an allowlisted model that this
tool had suspended earlier, so adding a model to the allowlist takes effect
without leaving the mode.
.P
The tool is normally started every minute by the
.I ctbrec-disk-mode.timer
systemd timer inside the container. Do not run it from the Proxmox host
directly; use
.BR ctbrec-disk-mode-remote (1)
for that.
.P
.SS Forced mode
.B \-\-on
turns the allowlist into an always\-on rule: the mode is entered and enforced
regardless of free space, so only allowlisted models record. Forced mode is
stored as a marker file in the state directory, so it survives reboots, ctbrec
restarts, timer runs, and
.BR "install \-\-keep\-config" .
.B \-\-off
removes the marker and leaves the mode, resuming the models this tool
suspended; the disk thresholds apply again on the next run. Turning forced mode
off does not disable disk protection: if free space is at or below the enter
threshold, the normal low\-space mode re\-enters on the next run. ctbrec's own
.B minimumSpaceLeftInBytes
reserve still stops everything, allowlisted models included, at the hard floor.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-config \fIPATH\fR
Configuration file. Default:
.IR /etc/ctbrec-disk-mode.json .
.TP
.B \-\-once
Evaluate the thresholds and enter, enforce, or exit the mode. This is the
default action when no action flag is given.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print free space, both thresholds, the ctbrec hard floor, the current mode,
and every allowlisted model with its active/paused state. Changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-on
Turn forced mode on and apply it immediately. The allowlist stays enforced
regardless of free space until
.B \-\-off
is given. Batching, pacing, and the failure backoff still apply, so a large
set of non\-allowlisted models is suspended over several runs at most
.B max_suspends_per_run
per run.
.TP
.B \-\-off
Turn forced mode off and leave the mode, resuming the models this tool
suspended, in the usual paced batches. Models you paused by hand are left
paused. The disk thresholds decide again on the next run.
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Force the mode off and resume the models this tool suspended, regardless of
free space. Resumes are batched like suspends, so a large set may need more than
one run; the mode stays active until the batch is drained. If forced mode is
on, the next run re\-enters the mode; use
.B \-\-off
to keep it off.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
With
.B \-\-once
or
.BR \-\-reset ,
print the intended suspend or resume actions without changing anything.
.TP
.B \-\-ensure-floor
Set ctbrec's own
.B minimumSpaceLeftInBytes
setting to the configured hard floor and exit. That is the native stop\-all
reserve, independent of the allowlist. The installer runs this once.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.SS Thresholds
.IP \(bu 2
Enter the mode when free space is less than or equal to
.BR enter_gib .
.IP \(bu 2
Exit the mode when free space is greater than or equal to
.BR exit_gib .
.IP \(bu 2
Between the two thresholds the mode keeps its current state. The gap is
hysteresis: without it the mode would flap while recordings consume space.
.SS State
The set of models suspended by this tool is stored in
.IR state.json .
A URL is added only after the API confirmed that suspend, so the file always
describes what the tool really changed, even when a run aborts halfway.
On exit, a stored model is resumed only when it is still suspended. A model
that the user resumed by hand, or that was already paused before the mode was
entered, is skipped. Resuming through the ctbrec API also clears the model's
``marked for later'' flag, because that is what the API's
.B resume
action does.
.SS Batched recorder actions
ctbrec's recorder deadlocks when a stop\-recording call joins a virtual thread
while holding the download monitor. A burst of hundreds of suspend calls turns
that single wedged call into a frozen recorder, an exhausted request pool, and
a stalled online monitor. This tool therefore:
.IP \(bu 2
suspends only models that are recording right now, taken from the API's
.B listCurrentlyRecording
action;
.IP \(bu 2
sends at most
.B max_suspends_per_run
suspend calls and
.B max_resumes_per_run
resume calls per run, with
.B action_delay_s
seconds between them;
.IP \(bu 2
never retries a suspend or resume that failed;
.IP \(bu 2
stops the batch when a single call takes longer than
.BR slow_action_s ,
so a struggling recorder is not hammered;
.IP \(bu 2
uses
.B action_timeout_s
for those calls, which is much shorter than the read timeout.
.SS Failure backoff
When a suspend or resume fails or times out, the run stops immediately, the
failure (time, model, error, retry time) is written to
.IR state.json ,
and no further recorder action is taken for
.B failure_cooldown_s
seconds. During the backoff the timer keeps running but only logs. When the
cooldown expires the tool tries again.
.P
If free space keeps falling while the tool is backing off, the ctbrec recorder
is most likely wedged and
.I ctbrec.service
has to be restarted; the log says so explicitly. A wedged recorder cannot be
recovered through the API.
.SS Concurrency
The tool does not change
.BR concurrentRecordings .
With the default value
.B 0
(unlimited) all allowlisted models record simultaneously. Limiting the
allowlist therefore limits recordings without touching global concurrency.
.P
If the allowlist contains duplicate entries for one model, for example the
same name on two sites, every matching model is treated as allowlisted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ctbrec-disk-mode.json
Configuration. Required keys:
.BR api_url ,
.BR enter_gib ,
.BR exit_gib ,
.BR allowlist .
Optional keys and their defaults:
.RS 4
.TP
.B hard_floor_gib
.BR 20 .
ctbrec's own
.B minimumSpaceLeftInBytes
reserve.
.TP
.B timeout
.BR 60 .
Read timeout in seconds.
.TP
.B action_timeout_s
.BR 15 .
Suspend and resume timeout in seconds.
.TP
.B max_suspends_per_run
.BR 60 .
Suspend calls per run.
.TP
.B max_resumes_per_run
.BR 60 .
Resume calls per run.
.TP
.B action_delay_s
.BR 0.2 .
Pause between those calls.
.TP
.B slow_action_s
.BR 5 .
Stop the batch early when a single call takes longer than this.
.TP
.B failure_cooldown_s
.BR 600 .
Backoff after a failed call.
.RE
.P
The allowlist is a list of model display names and is matched
case\-insensitively.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ctbrec-disk-mode/state.json
Active mode, entry timestamp, the URLs confirmed suspended by the tool, and the
last failure with its retry time. When systemd sets
.BR STATE_DIRECTORY ,
that directory is used instead.
.TP
.I /var/lib/ctbrec-disk-mode/forced
Marker file whose presence means forced mode is on. Created by
.B \-\-on
and removed by
.BR \-\-off .
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the current decision without acting:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-disk-mode \-\-status
.EE
.P
Run one evaluation by hand:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-disk-mode \-\-once
.EE
.P
See what entering the mode would suspend:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-disk-mode \-\-once \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.P
Leave the mode immediately:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-disk-mode \-\-reset
.EE
.P
Keep only the allowlist recording until further notice:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-disk-mode \-\-on
.EE
.P
Allow every model to record again:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-disk-mode \-\-off
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including ``nothing to do'' and ``backing off''.
.TP
.B 1
The ctbrec API was unreachable or returned an error outside a suspend or resume
batch, such as while reading the free space or the model list.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ctbrec-disk-mode-remote (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for local use.
